Why don't people like the many-worlds interpretation of quantum mechanics?

Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/ The many-worlds interpretation of quantum mechanics is often criticized for several reasons. One of the main criticisms is that it seems to contradict the principle of causality, which states that an event can only have one cause and one effect. In the many-worlds interpretation, every possible outcome of a quantum event occurs in a separate universe, which can lead to an infinite number of possible causes and effects. This can make it difficult to explain how the universe works and how events are related to each other. Additionally, the many-worlds interpretation has been criticized for being too complex and difficult to test, as it relies on the existence of parallel universes that cannot be observed directly. Despite these criticisms, the many-worlds interpretation remains a popular and influential theory in the field of quantum mechanics.

